1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,760
In March 2015, NASA sent astronaut Scott

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,520
Kelly to live on the International Space

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,190
Station for almost a year.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,950
His identical twin brother, Mark,

5
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,190
also an astronaut, stayed on Earth.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:12,890
That made them a perfect experiment.

7
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,850
Same DNA, two very different worlds.

8
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:18,920
340 days later, Scott came home taller,

9
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:21,850
with swollen legs and painful rashes.

10
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:23,560
Even some of his genes were behaving

11
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:24,290
differently.

12
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:25,560
So what does a year in space

13
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,310
actually do to your body?

14
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:29,560
Let's follow it, from launch day to

15
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:30,070
landing.

16
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:32,230
It starts fast.

17
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,080
Less than 10 minutes after liftoff,

18
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:35,480
the engines cut out, and you're in

19
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:35,950
orbit.

20
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,040
Here's the strange part: there's still plenty

21
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:39,370
of gravity up there.

22
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:41,690
At the station's height, it's about 90%

23
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:43,070
as strong as on the ground.

24
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:44,820
You float because the station is falling

25
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:48,130
around the Earth at 28,000 km/h,

26
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:49,670
and you're falling with it.

27
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,100
That's fast enough to circle the planet

28
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:52,370
every 90 minutes.

29
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,510
You'll see 16 sunrises every single day.

30
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,280
The first thing to go wrong is

31
00:00:58,280 --> 00:00:59,410
your sense of balance.

32
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,420
Your inner ear uses gravity to tell

33
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:02,370
which way is down.

34
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:03,810
Suddenly, there is no down,

35
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:06,150
and its signals stop making sense.

36
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:08,620
Depending on the study, somewhere between 40

37
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:11,300
and 80% of astronauts get space sick

38
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:12,830
in their first few days.

39
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,800
At the same time, your body fluids

40
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,210
start to move.

41
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:18,760
On Earth, gravity pulls them toward your

42
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:20,840
feet. In orbit, they spread up into

43
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:22,050
your chest and head.

44
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,140
On NASA's Skylab missions, astronauts lost more

45
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:26,940
than 2 liters of fluid from their

46
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:28,730
legs in just 3 days.

47
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:30,810
Astronauts call it puffy face,

48
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:33,370
bird legs. Your face swells,

49
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,640
your legs get thin, and your nose

50
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:38,110
feels blocked, like a permanent cold.

51
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,020
That blocked nose dulls your sense of

52
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:42,480
taste. It's one reason astronauts love hot

53
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:42,950
sauce.

54
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:45,810
Within days, you start to grow.

55
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:48,760
Without gravity squeezing it, your spine stretches

56
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:51,210
out, and its natural curve straightens.

57
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:54,190
Astronauts can grow up to 3% taller.

58
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,300
Scott Kelly came home almost 4 cm

59
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:57,030
taller.

60
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:59,340
But a stretched spine is an unhappy

61
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:01,680
one. Back pain is one of the

62
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:03,650
most common complaints in orbit,

63
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,140
and astronauts are about 4 times more

64
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:07,470
likely to slip a disc.

65
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,230
Everyday life gets weird, too.

66
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:11,330
Take crying.

67
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:12,810
Your tears can't fall.

68
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:14,760
They cling to your eye and grow

69
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:16,090
into a ball of water.

70
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:18,730
In 2001, during a spacewalk,

71
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:21,380
Chris Hadfield got anti-fog liquid in his

72
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:23,370
eye. The tears built up,

73
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,500
crossed his nose, and blinded his other

74
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:26,210
eye too.

75
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,200
He stayed calm, and once his eyes

76
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:30,410
cleared, he finished the spacewalk.

77
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:31,610
Burping is worse.

78
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:33,900
On Earth, gas rises to the top

79
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:34,670
of your stomach.

80
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:37,020
In orbit, it stays mixed with your

81
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:39,300
food, so a burp often brings food

82
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:40,090
up with it.

83
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,390
And sleep? There's no up or down.

84
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:44,260
You zip yourself into a sleeping bag

85
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:46,200
strapped to the wall, so you don't

86
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:47,550
drift around in the night.

87
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:50,300
The biggest changes are the ones you

88
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:51,090
can't see.

89
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:52,920
Your skeleton is built to carry your

90
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:55,350
weight. In orbit, it carries nothing,

91
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,610
so your body starts taking bone apart.

92
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:00,360
Astronauts can lose 1 to 1.5% of

93
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:01,820
the bone in their hips and spine

94
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,840
every month. On Earth, older people lose

95
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:06,350
about that much in a year.

96
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,790
Muscles waste away too.

97
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,840
In one study, astronauts' calf muscles shrank

98
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:13,670
by around 13% over 6 months.

99
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:14,750
And that's with exercise.

100
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:16,680
Crews work out for about 2 hours

101
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:19,180
every day, strapped to a treadmill with

102
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:21,200
bungee cords so they don't float off

103
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:21,570
it.

104
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:24,550
Your heart gets an easier job.

105
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:26,180
It no longer has to pump blood

106
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:27,570
uphill to your brain.

107
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:29,850
In a NASA study of 12 astronauts,

108
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:32,360
their hearts became about 9% more spherical

109
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:33,290
in orbit.

110
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,510
A rounder heart pumps less efficiently.

111
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:37,380
The good news: they went back to

112
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:39,550
their normal shape after landing.

113
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:41,950
Then there are your eyes.

114
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:43,600
All that fluid in your head presses

115
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:44,750
on them from behind.

116
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:47,180
Over months, the back of the eyeball

117
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:49,100
can flatten, and the nerve at the

118
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:50,670
back of your eye can swell.

119
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:52,780
About 2 in 3 astronauts on long

120
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:54,670
missions show early signs.

121
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:57,220
Their vision can shift, making nearby things

122
00:03:57,220 --> 00:03:58,650
harder to focus on.

123
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:00,840
NASA calls it SANS, and it's one

124
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,400
of the big health problems to solve

125
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:03,830
before a trip to Mars.

126
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:06,470
And then there's radiation.

127
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:08,990
On Earth, the thick atmosphere shields you.

128
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:11,420
On the station, you absorb roughly 100

129
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,810
times more radiation every day.

130
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,520
Six months on board adds up to

131
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:18,040
decades' worth of the natural background radiation

132
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:19,390
you'd get on the ground.

133
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,150
Some of it you can even see.

134
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,180
Close your eyes, and you may notice

135
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:24,590
tiny flashes of light.

136
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,920
Scientists think those are cosmic rays passing

137
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:29,250
through your eyes.

138
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:33,640
On March 1, 2016, Scott Kelly landed

139
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:34,710
back on Earth.

140
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:37,070
Then scientists compared him with Mark.

141
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:39,600
Scott's telomeres, the protective caps on the

142
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:41,640
ends of his chromosomes, had actually grown

143
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:42,870
longer in space.

144
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,570
Within days of landing, they shrank back,

145
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:47,730
and some ended up shorter than before.

146
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:49,320
Many of his genes had changed how

147
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:50,510
active they were.

148
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:51,950
Most went back to normal,

149
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:54,520
but some immune genes were still hyperactive

150
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:55,750
6 months later.

151
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:57,940
And on cognitive tests, he got slower

152
00:04:57,940 --> 00:04:59,870
and less accurate after landing,

153
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,230
and that lasted at least 6 months.

154
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:04,330
One more thing: he's technically younger.

155
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:06,410
Moving that fast slows time,

156
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:08,800
just slightly. Over all his days in

157
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:11,520
space, Scott has aged about 13 milliseconds

158
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:13,770
less than if he'd stayed home.

159
00:05:15,060 --> 00:05:16,160
Coming home is a shock of its

160
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:16,630
own.

161
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:18,650
All that fluid rushes back down.

162
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:20,300
Kelly said his legs swelled up,

163
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:22,220
and his joints and muscles ached for

164
00:05:22,220 --> 00:05:22,670
weeks.

165
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:24,580
He broke out in painful rashes where

166
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:26,110
his skin touched the bed.

167
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:27,610
After a year of floating,

168
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:29,630
it wasn't used to touching anything.

169
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:31,450
His height shrank right back.

170
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:33,910
Rebuilding bone and muscle took months,

171
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:35,710
and even 3 months later,

172
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:37,890
he still had sore feet and fatigue.

173
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:40,650
Most of these changes reverse.

174
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:42,940
The human body is remarkably good at

175
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:44,750
adapting, in both directions.

176
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:46,020
But a trip to Mars would take

177
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:48,620
years, not months, with no hospital and

178
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:49,950
no quick way home.

179
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:51,820
That's why NASA studied a pair of

180
00:05:51,820 --> 00:05:53,620
twins. Before we send people to another

181
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:55,460
planet, we need to know what space

182
00:05:55,460 --> 00:05:57,630
does to the one body they're bringing.

183
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,390
If you enjoyed this, subscribe.

184
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:01,140
There's a whole Shorts series on what

185
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:02,530
space does to your body,

186
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:04,470
one weird change at a time.

